METHOD
For each deterministic sample the audit chooses a finite half-level and
an arbitrary nonempty word of irreducible factors.  It constructs the
exact character-positive half-profile p, its multiplication matrix
P=N_p, and the fusion square d=p^2 with arbitrary-precision integers.

For every column pair it checks whether the minors

  Delta_R(i,j)=p_i P_(R,j)-p_j P_(R,i)

have a common sign over R.  It then checks every anchored current
against the exact Pluecker identity

  d_0(N_d)_(R,S)-d_Rd_S
    =sum_(i<j) Delta_R(i,j)Delta_S(i,j).

No current or identity fails.  Common coordinate signs, fixed ordinary
gaps, fixed index sums, fixed cyclic gaps, cyclic-gap suffixes, and
proper cyclic-gap prefixes all have exact failures.  The first proper
cyclic-prefix failure is the three-factor word recorded in OUTPUT.
For the first 1,000 samples, the audit also constructs the word matrix
independently by multiplying the irreducible fusion matrices.  It checks
every matrix entry and then checks the star boundary block of the induced
exterior-square Gram semigroup against the anchored current.  All 245,401
matrix entries and all 245,401 semigroup coordinates agree exactly.
The same independently accumulated factor words are also realized as
products of the cyclic triangular kernels τ_q.  All 14,267 gradient
coordinates, 245,401 cyclic kernel entries, and 245,401 cyclic current
identities agree exactly with the finite-fusion construction.
These are bounded route diagnostics; they are not an unbounded proof
of the complete Pluecker payment.

